# Screen naming

Name screens **entity first, then role**: `<Entity><Role>Screen`.

| Role | Purpose | Example |
|---|---|---|
| `Editor` | Create and edit (optional id) | `ExerciseEditorScreen` |
| `Details` | Read-only view of one item | `ExerciseDetailsScreen` |
| `Preview` | Summary before an action | `WorkoutPreviewScreen` |
| `Session` | Live, in-progress flow | `WorkoutSessionScreen` |

- Don't use `New`, `Create`, `Edit`, `Update` or other verbs in screen names; an `Editor` covers both create and edit.
- Every type for a screen shares its prefix: `<Name>Screen`, `<Name>ScreenContent`, `<Name>ViewModel`, `<Name>ScreenState`, `<Name>Error`, `<Name>Event`, `<Name>Destination`, plus files `<Name>Screen.kt` and `<Name>Navigation.kt`.
- String keys use the snake_case prefix: `exercise_editor_*`, `exercise_details_*`.
- Reusable components inside a feature are named for what they are, not for the screen they first appeared on (`TrackWeightToggle`, not `NewExerciseTrackWeightToggle`).

## Navigation

- Helpers are named after the screen they open: `NavGraphBuilder.exerciseEditorScreen()`, `NavController.navigateToExerciseEditor(...)`.
- Editors take a nullable id where `null` means create, not overloads or `""`:

```kotlin
// ❌ BAD
data class NewExerciseDestination(val exerciseId: String = "")
fun NavController.navigateToNewExercise(exerciseId: String? = null) { ... }
fun NavController.navigateToNewExercise(exerciseId: UUID) { ... }

// ✅ GOOD
data class ExerciseEditorDestination(val exerciseId: String? = null)
fun NavController.navigateToExerciseEditor(exerciseId: UUID? = null) {
    navigate(ExerciseEditorDestination(exerciseId = exerciseId?.toString()))
}
```

- Callbacks are named after what the user wants to do: `onNavigateToCreateExercise`, `onNavigateToEditExercise(id)`, `onNavigateToExerciseDetails(id)`, `onNavigateUp`. Wire them to helpers in `AppRoot.kt`.
